From: Local build <local@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] FIX: renderloop: frames shown by direct scanout have a render journal of their own (KWIN_RENDER_JOURNAL_SCANOUT)

The render time estimate is the smoothed render time plus twice the largest recent excess over it, and that excess
decays over seconds. A frame whose content is a client's buffer on a plane takes 0.1-0.3 ms; a rendered frame
counts as 2 ms at least. In one journal, the first rendered frame after a stretch of direct scanout - a window
closing over the desktop, a popup over a fullscreen client - is a 1.8 ms excess: the estimate goes from 0.2 to
~5.2 ms, the compositor plans its frames two refreshes ahead, and for the next seconds a fullscreen client's frames
reach the screen a refresh late. Bare keyflip on a 240 Hz output (2026-10-19): the ~30 keys after each such
moment took 11-14 ms instead of 7-10; p50 / p90 10.4 / 13.6 ms against 9.6 / 10.9 when nothing was rendered between
runs. Upstream 6.7 has the same split (the 2 ms minimum is in the OpenGL render time query); 6.8's 5338c2e9a7
avoided it by counting direct scanout frames as 2 ms too, which plans every direct scanout frame ~1.8 ms earlier
(our FIX-F undoes that part).

Now each kind has a journal: frames with rendered content, and frames whose content was scanned out (the compositor
marks them, OutputFrame::setContentScannedOut). The next frame is estimated from the kind of the last frame with
content; frames with neither keep it: one where only the cursor was rendered is skipped (as before), one with nothing
at all counts in that journal with its own short time, as in the single journal (v3: skipping those raised the
rendered frames' estimate on the laptop's 780M at 144 Hz into triple buffering, one repeat per launcher opening). The
first frame of the other kind is planned with the last kind's estimate, as one journal would have planned it before
the excess. Each frame counts as a skip in the other kind's journal, so that one's excess decays with time as
it would have in one journal (v4: frozen through direct scanout, the rendered frames' excess kept the laptop's 780M
triple buffered when the next composited animation began, +1.2 launcher repeats a second).
When the content is a client's buffer again, the compositor also leaves triple buffering at once: the rendered
frames' estimate may have switched it there, and its hysteresis kept it for 10 more frames (v2, 2026-10-19: on the
laptop at 144 Hz the estimate stayed at 0.4 ms, but the first ~10 keys after a composited moment were a vblank late).
KWIN_RENDER_JOURNAL_SCANOUT=0 restores the single journal; $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-render-journal-scanout holding 0 or
1 overrides the variable while it exists (read at most once a second: an A/B in a running host session).
KWIN_RENDER_ESTIMATE_UPSTREAM=1 keeps the single journal as well.

---

diff --git a/src/compositor.cpp b/src/compositor.cpp
index f02e003..a3db4d4 100644
--- a/src/compositor.cpp
+++ b/src/compositor.cpp
@@ -930,6 +930,10 @@ void Compositor::composite(RenderLoop *renderLoop)
 
     const auto renderLayers = [&]() -> std::expected<void, OutputError> {
         for (auto &layer : layers) {
+            if (layer.view->layer()->isEnabled() && layer.directScanout && !layer.cursor) {
+                // for the render time estimate: this frame's content is a client's buffer
+                frame->setContentScannedOut();
+            }
             if (!layer.view->layer()->needsRepaint()) {
                 continue;
             }
